------MODEL ARCHITECTURE------

A small ResNet-style CNN trained from random initialization, 2.8M parameters.
Instead of starting with a large kernel and MaxPool to shrink the size of the input image,
I chose to use a small 3x3 filter with a relatively small width and high depth,
because a lot of filters would make the net very wide very soon, which means a lot of
parameters which we want to avoid. Because there are only 16,000 Images for training
(I Chose 80% train 20% validation) a net with tens of millions of parameters would probably
achieve overfit on the set.
we are trying to distinguish between 20 bird classes that look very similar,
and the differences lie in tiny details like the shape of the beak or neck.
If I had shrunk the image right at the start (downsampling),
I would have thrown away this crucial, hard to notice information.

I limited the width: the number of filters starts at 32 and
gradually doubles to 64, 128, and up to a maximum of 256 channels.
To ensure the network is still smart enough to learn complex patterns
despite being narrow, I used depth, the image passes through 4 processing
stages, each containing 2 convolution blocks. Across these stages, the
physical spatial resolution (the pixels)  drops from 112 to 56, to 28, and finally to 14.
To prevent vanishing gradient in the backpropogation here
each block includes Skip Connection.

At the end, I have global average pooling in order to prevent overfit (the model decides based on
the features and not their specific location in the image) , dropout at 0.2, and a single linear layer to
20 logits.


-----TRAINING STRATEGY-------
To train the model, I used a batch size of 64,
a learning rate of 3e-4, cross-entropy with label
smoothing of 0.1, and trained for 30 epochs. I chose 30
epochs because the clean validation accuracy does not really changes around
epoch 25, so going further is pointless.

Small training dataset would lead the model to find 'shortcuts', like relying only on the bird's color.
I looked into standard machine learning best practices that prevent a model from memorizing.
I chose AdamW because it has a built-in mechanism called Weight Decay.
This mechanism penalizes decay the weights to 0 at every learning step, so if for example we got a single weight
that memorized a specific image of a flamingo, it will help the model solve a flamingo picture but will keep shrinking on
the other images, but a "generalized" weight that recognize a shape or a beak will keep getting score each image

the learning rate needs to decrease as we get closer to the end of the training.
Cosine Annealing allows the model to make very tiny adjustments to its weights without ruining what it already learned.
Instead of guessing when to lower the rate, I used Cosine Annealing,
which smoothly reduces the step size. This helped the model stabilize optimally.





---------EVALUATION METHODOLOGY---------

To evaluate the model, I split the 20,000 training images into 16,000 for
training (80%) and 4,000 for validation (20%). I made sure this split was balanced,
meaning every bird class contributed exactly 800 images to training and 200 to validation.
I also used a fixed random seed to ensure this exact split remained identical across all my experiments,
so any change in accuracy is purely due to the model's performance, not a lucky data split.

The augmentations/ folder contains only 20 images per class, too few
to measure with. So instead of using those  images, I wrote code to recreate three types of image manipulations:
Black and White, Color Jitter,Salt-and-Pepper Noise.
Important - when recreating the manipulations i made sure it's accurate to the augmentations folder
meaning i corrupted around the same % of pixels of the images in salt and pepper
I changed the contrast saturation and brighness at similar scale range and I created same black and white images.
Then, I applied these manipulations to my entire set of 4,000 validation images.
This gave me three full-sized "stress sets" to properly test how robust my model really is
The color changing is selected from a given range which randomly gives images cold/warm manipulation.
the image path used as the seed so the randomness is across the entire dataset, meaning each specific image will always
get the same random manipulation accross al test runs.

-------HELD-OUT MANIPULATION--------

The final test uses manipulations we cannot see. A model trained on the three
manipulations we do have and evaluated on those same three would produce a
number that predicts nothing -- it would only show that the network learned
what it was shown.

so I held out salt-and-pepper noise entirely. It never appears in
training. It is the only honest evidence we have
about generalization to an unseen manipulation, and as reported below it is
what disqualified  exp2 and exp3.

salt-and-pepper as the held-out because it works by a
different mechanism from the other two. Black and White and color jitter both
change pixel values smoothly and globally. salt-and-pepper completly change isolated
pixels. If the model trains on the two above and still succeed in the salt and pepper test
its the best proof to the model robustness.

--------------EXPERIMENTS------------

                        baseline    exp 1     exp 2     exp 3
  clean                   79.97     79.95     79.97     78.40
  black_white             34.60     69.57     68.93     72.75
  color_jitter            76.15     78.43     78.83     77.82
  salt_pepper (held out)  43.43     25.75     23.20     13.33
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  stress mean             51.39     57.92     56.98     54.63

Baseline:
I trained a basic model using only standard cropping and flipping.
This revealed a weakness: low score black-and-white images.The reason is that the model found a
shortcut it relied almost entirely on color to identify birds instead of learning their actual shapes.
It also lost 37 points on salt-and-pepper
noise because the corrupted pixels created thousands of "fake edges" that
confused the network's early layers.The corrupted pixels are only 3% of the image, but each one is an extreme jump
against its neighbours, and the early convolutional layers respond precisely
to sharp local changes because that is how they detect edges. The noise
enters the network as thousands of false edges.


Experiment 1
Here i tried to make sure the model does not relay on the color
I applied random augmentations:  With a probability of 30% (p=0.3),
the image was converted to black/white .with 50% (p=0.5) the image went to Color Jitter
Those changes applied randomaly each epoch so the model never sees the exact same image twice.
It gets an unlimited number of variants of the same bird.
Now, the network is forced to strengthen the features that remain
This strategy was successful for its intended purpose accuracy on
black-and-white images doubled (from 34.6% to 69.6%), and the clean image accuracy did not drop at all.
The Trade-off:
because the model transferred its dependency from color onto shapes and edges,
it became much more vulnerable to things that corrupt shapes and
edges. And so, the result on the held-out salt-and-pepper noise
(which destroys edge pixels) fell from 43.4% down to 25.8%.  I ultimately submitted Experiment 1
because it offered the best average stress score, lost nothing on clean accuracy.

Experiment 2

experiment 1 plus Gaussian pixel noise

This supposed to solve the salt and pepper drop from exp1.but it failed salt-and-pepper dropped further, to 23.2, and nothing else improved.

Why it Failed:
Gaussian noise is gentle - the model learns to ignore gentle noises.
but the salt pepper noise is extreme and completely "deletes" a pixel
By training the network against gentle noise, I actually taught it
that small deviations are meaningless, so when the model have seen extreme deviations in the salt and pepper
it realized its something meaningful and the model became more sensitive to them instead of ignoring.
Robustness to noise is different accross noise types.

Experiment 3

Here i gave up on what i tried in exp2, and tried to amplify what i did in exp1 that actually worked
i increased the probabilities of black and white from 30% to 50% and the color jitter to 70%.
The black and white score improved slightly but the other scores got worse, the model now is
even more so relaying on the shapes which obviously will make the salt pepper go down as mentioned before.

In General
Less Color dependecy = more shape dependency = score on salt and pepper goes down.


We submit experiment 1. It has the best stress mean, loses nothing on clean
accuracy, and among the configurations we tried it is the least brittle on
the manipulation it never saw.


A NOTE ON MEASUREMENT NOISE

Exact same run of experiment 1 from scratch with identical settings to produce the
submitted weights. Clean, black and white and color jitter accuracy remaained the same to a differenc of
0.7 points change but Salt-and-pepper moved from 32.5 to 25.8.

That is a 7-point change between two runs that are different only in random
initialization, which means the salt and pepper number is  noisier than
the other three. The overall change across experiments (43.4 down to 13.3)
is too large to be noise. I report the second run's numbers above, since
those what i got in the second run of exp1.


KNOWN WEAKNESS

The submitted model is more sensitive to sparse extreme pixel corruption than
the baseline was. If the final test includes a manipulation of that kind, It
will lose accuracy there.

I accepted this knowingly. The challenge description specifies background,
lighting and color manipulations, and on those the  model is
better than the baseline with no loss on clean images.
